================================================================================
T-139 — negative control: does the fixture actually produce the data it claims?
================================================================================

A behaviour task's control removes the fix and watches the tests fail. That shape
does not apply to a measurement harness, because nothing is being fixed. The
honest control here is the question that would sink the task if it went unasked:

    a generator that silently wrote a hundred rows instead of a hundred and
    forty-six thousand would produce a fast, plausible, completely meaningless
    number, and would pass every other check in the harness.

Four controls answer it. All four are in
backend/src/test/java/org/iskcon/kms/perf/TempleScaleFixtureControlIT.java.

  1. Every table counted with count(*) and compared against arithmetic that
     lives in TempleScale, beside the parameters, NOT inside the generator —
     so the generator cannot agree with itself.
  2. The size parameter is doubled and the ledger doubles with it. A generator
     that ignored its parameters passes control 1 and fails this.
  3. Rows are deleted behind the check's back and the check is shown FAILING.
     Without this, "the counts matched" has no evidence that anything was
     capable of not matching.
  4. Two tenants generated side by side, each counted as the unprivileged
     application role with app.tenant_id set. CLAUDE.md's first trap is that a
     fixture seeded as a superuser proves nothing about the query the
     application runs.

Command:

  export JAVA_HOME=/opt/homebrew/opt/openjdk@21/libexec/openjdk.jdk/Contents/Home
  tools/work-lock.sh run verify 'cd backend && KMS_PERF=1 ./gradlew --no-daemon \
      test --tests "*TempleScaleFixtureControlIT*"'

