--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
READING IT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Control 1: eleven tables, counted from the database, every one equal to what the
           parameters said. recipe_ingredients = 8 recipes x 5 lines = 40.
           stock_movements = 40 opening receipts + 30 days x 80 = 2,440.
           meal_plans COOKED = 30 x 9 dishes + 2 events = 272.

Control 2: 30 days gives 2,440 movements, 60 days gives 4,840. The daily churn
           doubles exactly (2,400 -> 4,800); the 40 opening receipts do not
           scale with history and are asserted separately rather than folded
           into a round 2x that would quietly be wrong.

Control 3: THE ONE THAT MATTERS. 1,000 rows deleted as the superuser — which is
           also the only role that can, because stock_movements is append-only
           to the application — and verify() then refuses:

               stock_movements: counted 1440, expected 2440 (short by 1000)

           That refusal is what stops a fast, meaningless number reaching the
           proof file. It is asserted with assertThatThrownBy, so the control
           fails if the check ever stops having teeth.

Control 4: Temple A (30 days) counts 2,440 movements and Temple B (5 days)
           counts 440, each read as kms_app with its own app.tenant_id. If RLS
           were absent, or the fixture had been seeded as a superuser, both
           would report 2,880 — the sum of the two temples.

RESULT: 4 tests, 4 passed, 0 failed.
